WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.390
My name is David Aja.

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:10.950
I'm a member of the Solutions
Engineering team at RStudio.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.000
Or, if you prefer,
I posit solutions.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.290
I help data scientists get
their ideas into production,

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:20.850
and in the course
of doing that work,

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.160
I see a lot of broken things.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.950
And so that's why today I'll
be talking about why I think

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:29.050
you should use renv... probably.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.750
So there are two
parts to this talk.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.550
The first part is
going to be about why

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:36.675
I think you should do this.

00:00:36.675 --> 00:00:39.060
And then in the second
part, we'll talk about how.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.160
But let's start with why.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.630
And I think the reason that
I'm kind of up here giving

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.570
this talk is because I think
we have a standards problem.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:51.180
And the way I would express
that standards problem is

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.000
that I don't know if we have
a good answer to the following

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.620
question, which is
what makes a project

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:01.200
an R project?
We're probably going

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.090
to need to define
some terms in order

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:04.690
to make our way through that.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:06.390
So let's start with project.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.130
What is a project?

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.750
This is the definition you
get if you Google it, right.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.290
We've got an individual or
a collaborative enterprise

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:14.890
aimed at a particular aim.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.460
I think that's a pretty
helpful definition,

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.950
but sometimes I like
to try to define

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:21.400
things functionally right.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.910
A thing is what it does.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:26.700
And so what do projects do?

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.590
Projects ship.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.540
This is a slightly
unusual way of talking.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:35.670
It's kind of a legacy of our
history of writing software

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:39.170
to physical media and then
mailing it to your house.

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.485
But this is kind of
the paradigm that we

00:01:41.485 --> 00:01:43.860
have in mind when we talk
about shipping projects, right?

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.390
We've worked on something.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.090
I have it.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:47.170
I need to get it to you.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:51.450
And I want you to have
a particular experience.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.050
Some of you may be thinking
about a particular like file

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:57.750
extension that maybe
is like you're like it

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.500
says that kind of thing.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:00.100
And there.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.580
And so I'll just say
like the, I think

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.990
the, .Rproj or
RStudio project file

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:09.789
is not going to be sufficient
for our purposes here.

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:12.990
And part of the reason that's
true is because what's in the

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:17.220
RStudio project file is editor
configuration and editor editor

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.380
configuration is
important to you.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.440
But when you send data analysis
results, data, data analysis

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.390
results to someone,
they typically

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:28.200
don't ask you how many spaces
you put in a tab, right?

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:32.260
And so the idea is you don't
ship your editor. A thing

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:34.920
I think it's important
to say, all right,

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.170
I'm making a lot of
declarative statements

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:41.730
here, like part of this is from
a history of screwing things

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:43.180
up pretty catastrophically.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.840
So I have actually
shipped my editor

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.110
and then I ended up debugging
my code in our general counsel's

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:49.900
office.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.240
And that's not where I
want any of you to be.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.900
Right? so I think we have
to look for some better

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.350
ways of doing work.

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:57.960
All right.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.460
So the RStudio project file
is going to be necessary

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:03.363
if you use RStudio.

00:03:03.363 --> 00:03:05.280
And I think an important
thing to acknowledge,

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.738
even though this
is RStudio::conf

00:03:06.738 --> 00:03:09.420
is that not everyone
does use RStudio.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.850
The RStudio project
file is necessary,

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:13.230
but for the purpose
of helping us

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.270
understand what a
project is, it's

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:18.330
not going to be sufficient.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:21.180
So we have to look elsewhere
for a definition of what

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:22.620
a project is.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:25.410
And one of the things that I
found really inspiring when

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:29.010
I was trying to figure out
how to more reliably deliver

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:32.760
software, was a website
called 12factor.net.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.260
This website was
published by people

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.840
who work on the deployment
platform, Heroku,

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.360
which helped people deploy
a software as a service

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.370
applications to the public.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:45.450
But a lot of the lessons that
they publish there, I think,

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:48.370
are useful even in the
data professional context.

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:50.130
And one of the things
they really stress

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:52.840
is the importance of
environment isolation, right?

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:56.550
So the 12 factor apps never
rely on the implicit existence

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.000
of system wide packages.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.100
And there are a couple
of things that they

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.120
say are important to
making this happen, right?

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.780
So you have to declare
all your dependencies

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.030
and then you have to isolate
them from other things

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:07.000
on the system.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.750
And you have to make sure that
no implicit dependencies leak

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:10.300
in.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:12.870
And I want to dwell on the
leak in thing for a moment,

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:16.170
because I'm going to give
a sort of stylized example

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:19.019
from my past to show you how
this kind of thing can happen.

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:22.560
Right? so let's say,
you know, or let's say

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.600
I was working on
a project, I did

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:27.510
a lot of work on the 2020
census for an advertising agency

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:31.440
I worked on at the time, and
I delivered some dashboard

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.270
and people looked at the
results and it was good.

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:39.030
And then someone asked me
to do some data analysis

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:41.470
on the census tract level.

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:43.290
It needed to do
between a 1 and 7

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:47.010
day look back there, like
80,000 census tracts.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:49.470
We were doing data
for, data analysis

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:52.590
for, at that point, several
months worth of data.

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:54.940
And so I was like, OK,
I need to do something.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:58.950
This might be a good case
for the latest dtplyr.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:01.500
And so I installed dtplyr.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.840
I was able to work
on the analysis

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:07.240
and everything was fine.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:11.650
Except I accidentally
by installing dtplyr,

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:13.930
upgraded the version
of the dplyr that

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:15.343
was being used in my dashboard.

00:05:15.343 --> 00:05:17.260
Because the thing I
haven't highlighted so far

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:20.520
is that we have a dependency
on a shared user library.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:28.640
The answer is not in production.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.940
And so I think I'm
going to borrow

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:34.070
Julia's excellent
metaphor from yesterday.

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:36.890
We want to find a way
of working that stops it

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:40.910
from being possible for us
to lock our keys in the car.

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:43.670
So let's talk about
how we do that, right?

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:47.053
The first step is going to be
to isolate your dependencies.

00:05:47.053 --> 00:05:48.470
And so in order
to do that, you're

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:51.170
going to need to
install the renv package

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:52.950
and then you're going
to initialize it.

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:54.783
You have a couple of
options for doing that.

00:05:54.783 --> 00:05:56.190
You can call the init function.

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:57.770
There's also a
little box you can

00:05:57.770 --> 00:06:00.650
check as you're creating a
new project in the rstudio IDE

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:03.980
and that will create and
this will sort of overwrite

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:05.090
your .libPaths, right?

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:08.900
So by default you have kind
of a user library and a system

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:11.750
library and renv will take
that over and say, OK,

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:14.960
now you have a project
specific library and then

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.260
your system library.

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:19.010
And so what that looks
like, if we go back in time,

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:20.970
I properly isolate my libraries.

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:24.080
I have this user
library and my dashboard

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:27.110
and then the project library
gets the upgraded versions,

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:27.720
right?

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.680
And that means that if I start
working on new projects right,

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:34.760
say I start doing experimental
things with new packages

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:35.760
and tidymodels.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:38.720
I'm not really concerned
about breaking other things

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:39.345
in my workflow.

00:06:39.345 --> 00:06:41.470
Right and the other thing
that's nice to note here,

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:43.910
right, is that you can adopt
this incrementally, right?

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:46.950
So you don't have to move
all of your projects at once.

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.340
You can see that my
dashboard is still

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:50.940
pointing in my user library.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.550
I can go and upgrade
that surgically

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:54.590
later on if I want to.

00:06:54.590 --> 00:06:56.990
And you might be asking
yourself, OK, well, you're

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:59.370
making me maintain
this isolated library.

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:01.520
Does that mean I need to
reinstall every package

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:02.910
from scratch each time?

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:04.180
The answer is no.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:06.060
renv maintains
a package cache.

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:08.720
So once you install the
package, it's available to you

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:12.442
to reuse in other projects if
they have the same dependency.

00:07:12.442 --> 00:07:14.900
So that first installation
might take a little bit of time,

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:20.420
but the subsequent installations
will go much faster.

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:23.330
Once we've isolated the
dependencies on our system,

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:25.970
the next thing we need to do
is to discover what they are

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:27.990
and write that down
somewhere, right?

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:30.470
And so calling the renv
snapshot function

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:33.050
is going to give us some
information about the versions

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:36.170
of packages we use
and which ones we have,

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:38.190
where we got those
packages from,

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:44.682
because as we heard, shared
drives RStudio Package Manager,

00:07:44.682 --> 00:07:46.640
r-universe, all of
these are different places

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:49.100
where you can get R
packages and so you

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:51.620
want to be able to keep
track of where you got them

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:54.223
as well as the version of R you
were using for your project.

00:07:54.223 --> 00:07:55.640
Right and so you
can see here this

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:59.165
is a package I've installed
from the r-universe,

00:07:59.165 --> 00:08:01.040
and we're so keeping
track of the information

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:02.670
about the Git commits, right?

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:06.170
And we're writing all of this
down in the renv lock file.

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:07.760
And I think the
renv.lock file

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:11.797
is the project artifact
that we're looking for.

00:08:11.797 --> 00:08:13.880
Part of the thing that is
going to enable us to do

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:16.070
is to collaborate
more effectively.

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:18.830
And so renv is itself very
helpful in giving you

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:21.607
status messages about
what you need to do next.

00:08:21.607 --> 00:08:23.690
And this is going to be
really helpful for context

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:25.782
where either you're
picking up an old project

00:08:25.782 --> 00:08:27.740
or you're sharing a
project with a teammate who

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:30.990
doesn't have the same package
set on their computer.

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:34.580
So you can see in this case,
I've installed some packages

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:37.760
or I've pulled down a
lock file and the packages

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:40.140
are in the lock file, but
they're not yet on my system.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:42.740
And renv is going
to tell me what to do.

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:44.300
This is a similar
kind of situation

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:47.520
where I started using a package,
but I haven't installed it yet.

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:49.910
Calling renv::status()
is again going to tell me

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:52.290
what to do to get right.

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:55.190
Right and if I remove a
package from my project,

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:57.770
then I want to remove those
dependencies as well because I

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:00.410
don't want to ship unnecessary
things to my deployment

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:02.520
target, whatever that is.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:04.980
Right and so this is going
to be the workflow, right?

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:07.100
We're going to initialize
a project in renv

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:08.930
we'll install the
packages we need,

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:11.270
we snapshot, we ship
and then later we

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:13.310
can restore that
environment once we

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:15.480
have the project in renv.

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:20.240
Right install snapshot
ship and restore.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:23.060
I did say "probably"
at the beginning

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:25.340
there are a couple of
things where this may not

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:26.310
apply to you.

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:29.330
One, I'll call it a bad
argument and one better one.

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:31.172
I don't intend to
reuse this code.

00:09:31.172 --> 00:09:31.880
This is too much.

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:34.280
This is too much overhead.

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:36.560
I think one of the
things about being

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:39.080
a data professional, right,
is that you are in fact, going

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:40.230
to have to reuse the code.

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:42.050
Right someone is
going to ask you, hey,

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:44.490
I got numbers from finance
that don't match yours.

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:45.240
What's going on?

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:46.140
Can you rediscover that?

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:47.557
And you're just
like, you're going

00:09:47.557 --> 00:09:49.800
to be in that
cycle of iteration.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:52.670
And so this is a tweet
I pulled from somebody

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:54.350
who is talking
about this as I was

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:55.830
avoiding working on this talk.

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:58.520
And I think it really perfectly
encapsulates that, like,

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:00.560
if you just treat things
like they're throwaway,

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:02.870
they're going to come back
and you should just work

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:05.750
like your work is valuable.

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:08.420
If you are purely a
package developer,

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:11.300
then you are in a somewhat
different circumstance

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:16.130
because the execution target is
not necessarily getting someone

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:17.490
to call your Plumber API.

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:19.970
It's CRAN reverse
dependency checks.

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:24.140
renv does have some
tools that you can use

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:25.640
to isolate environments
if you need

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:29.353
to do specialized testing and
the article on that in the renv

00:10:29.353 --> 00:10:30.770
documentation is
pretty excellent,

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:33.630
as is the rest of
renv's documentation.

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:37.610
So in summary, I think we have
an opportunity here to coalesce

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:38.870
on a project standard.

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:41.120
I encourage you
all to use renv

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:44.030
and I want to say a special
Thank you to Kevin,

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:46.970
without whose work, none
of this would be possible.

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:48.670
Thank you.

